Given a query like this:

```graphql
mutation CreateUser($input: CreateUserInput!, $productInput: CreateProductInput!) {
  createUser(input: $input) {
    email
    withProduct(input: $productInput) {
      name
      createdBy {
        email
      }
    }
  }
}
```

With variables like this:

```json
{
  "input": {
    "email": "user@example.com",
    "name": "User",
    "password": "Password"
  },
  "productInput": {
    "name": "Product Name",
    "sku": "12345"
  }
}
```

It creates a user and a product via federated services, returning a response like this:

```json
{
  "data": {
    "createUser": {
      "email": "user@example.com",
      "withProduct": {
        "name": "Product",
        "createdBy": {
          "email": "user@example.com"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Note that `CreateProductInput` was only defined in products subschema, and that `User` was extended there with the query field `withProduct(input: CreateProductInput!): Product!` as well.

Fixes a soundness issue I found in the original [kbremap](https://github.com/timokroeger/kbremap/blob/main/src/keyboard_hook.rs) code.

Drop code is not guaranteed to run (e.g. when using `mem::forget`).
Thus we cannot rely on drop unregistering the hook.
Make the hook abstraction require a static lifetime for the closure to ensure crash free operation in any case.

Program to reproduce the issue:
``` Rust
fn main() {
    {
        let mut pressed_keys = Vec::with_capacity(1);
        let hook = KeyboardHook::set_input_cb(|ev| {
            pressed_keys.push(ev);
            false
        });
        mem::forget(hook);
    }

    unsafe {
        let mut msg = mem::zeroed();
        GetMessageW(&mut msg, ptr::null_mut(), 0, 0);
    }
}
```

If assignment aligning is happening for a group of variable definition lines, and a line with multiple definitions is encountered, then align_assign.c will not align the most recent assignments before that line.

Without this fix, the unit test fails like:
```cpp
int foo(void) {
	unsigned a = 123;
	unsigned abcde = 456;
	unsigned abcdefghijklmnop = 789;
	unsigned w, x, y, z;
	unsigned u          = 16730;
	unsigned vvvvv      = 4917;
	unsigned wwwwwwwwww = 12428;
}
```

